## Files
- lib/atproto.ts (modify)

## What to do
The loopback (development) client_id embeds the redirect_uri in the URL itself per RFC 8252. Currently it only includes the normal callback URI. When ePDS is enabled, the ePDS login route uses a different redirect_uri (/api/oauth/epds/callback) which must also be embedded in the loopback client_id for the PAR request to be accepted.

### Problem
In development mode, the client_id looks like:
`http://localhost?scope=atproto+transition%3Ageneric&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A3000%2Fapi%2Foauth%2Fcallback`

The ePDS PAR request sends redirect_uri=/api/oauth/epds/callback, but this URI isn't in the loopback client_id. The ATProto spec for loopback clients requires that redirect_uris be embedded in the client_id URL.

### Fix
When NEXT_PUBLIC_EPDS_URL is set, the loopback client_id must include BOTH redirect URIs. The ATProto loopback spec allows multiple redirect_uri params:
`http://localhost?scope=...&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A3000%2Fapi%2Foauth%2Fcallback&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%3A3000%2Fapi%2Foauth%2Fepds%2Fcallback`

Modify DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId construction:
```typescript
const epdsEnabled = !!process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_EPDS_URL;
const redirectUris = [`${PUBLIC_URL}/api/oauth/callback`];
if (epdsEnabled) {
  redirectUris.push(`${PUBLIC_URL}/api/oauth/epds/callback`);
}

// For loopback client_id, embed all redirect URIs
const clientIdParams = new URLSearchParams();
clientIdParams.set('scope', OAUTH_SCOPE);
for (const uri of redirectUris) {
  clientIdParams.append('redirect_uri', uri);
}
const devClientId = `http://localhost?${clientIdParams.toString()}`;
```

Update DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG to use this new clientId. PROD_OAUTH_CONFIG doesn't need changes (it uses the metadata URL as client_id, and the metadata endpoint already handles the redirect_uris array from task 4).

Also update DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG.redirectUri — this is the default redirect URI for the normal flow and should stay as-is (`${PUBLIC_URL}/api/oauth/callback`). The ePDS flow uses its own redirect URI from getEpdsRedirectUri().

## Don't
- Change PROD_OAUTH_CONFIG
- Change the OAUTH_SCOPE
- Change the sessionStore or stateStore setup (those were modified in task 3)
- Modify any other exports

## Files
- lib/atproto.ts (modify)
- lib/epds/config.ts (modify)

## What to do

### Problem
When running locally with ngrok (or any tunnel), the user sets NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL to their ngrok URL (e.g. https://abc123.ngrok-free.app). But two functions ignore this:

1. **resolvePublicUrl()** in lib/atproto.ts checks NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL LAST. The priority order is: VERCEL_BRANCH_URL → VERCEL_URL → isDev (returns 127.0.0.1) → NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL. Since isDev is true when running locally, it always returns http://127.0.0.1:3000 and never reaches the NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL check.

2. **getEpdsClientId()** in lib/epds/config.ts checks `NODE_ENV === 'development'` and returns the loopback client_id. With ngrok, NODE_ENV is still 'development' but the app is publicly accessible — it needs the production-style client_id.

### Fix for resolvePublicUrl()

Move NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL to the TOP of the priority chain. It's an explicit override and should always win:

```typescript
export const resolvePublicUrl = (): string => {
  // 1. Explicit override — always wins (ngrok, custom domain, etc.)
  if (process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL) {
    return process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL;
  }
  // 2. Vercel auto-detection
  if (process.env.VERCEL_BRANCH_URL) {
    return `https://${process.env.VERCEL_BRANCH_URL}`;
  }
  if (process.env.VERCEL_URL) {
    return `https://${process.env.VERCEL_URL}`;
  }
  // 3. Local development fallback
  if (isDev) {
    return `http://127.0.0.1:${process.env.PORT ?? 3000}`;
  }
  throw new Error(
    'Set NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL, or deploy to Vercel'
  );
};
```

### Fix for getEpdsClientId()

Instead of checking NODE_ENV, check whether the resolved URL is actually a loopback address. This way ngrok URLs get the production client_id even in development:

```typescript
export function getEpdsClientId(): string {
  const publicUrl = resolvePublicUrl();
  const isLoopback = publicUrl.includes('127.0.0.1') || publicUrl.includes('localhost');
  if (isLoopback) {
    return DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId;
  }
  return `${publicUrl}/client-metadata.json`;
}
```

### Also update the JSDoc comment on resolvePublicUrl()

The existing comment says NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is priority 1 but the code has it as the last check. Fix the code to match the comment.

## Don't
- Change the Vercel URL detection logic
- Change PROD_OAUTH_CONFIG or DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG themselves
- Change any other functions in these files
- Add new environment variables

Review of bumicerts-2-4vq.6 found: app/api/oauth/epds/callback/route.ts lines 163-176 use sessionStore.get(tokenData.sub) which returns a NodeSavedSession (plain data object), then pass it to new Agent(session as any). Agent expects an OAuthSession (which has methods like signOut(), getTokenInfo(), etc.), not a NodeSavedSession. The Agent constructor will fail or produce a broken agent, causing describeRepo() to throw. The catch block silently falls back to resolvedHandle = tokenData.sub (the DID). Result: every ePDS login succeeds but the user's handle is always their DID (e.g. did:plc:abc123) instead of their human-readable handle (e.g. alice.climateai.org). Login works but UX is degraded — the handle shown in the UI will be a DID string.

Evidence: The normal callback (app/api/oauth/callback/route.ts line 33) correctly uses new Agent(session) where session is an OAuthSession returned by atprotoSDK.callback(). The ePDS callback should use atprotoSDK.restoreSession(tokenData.sub) after writing to sessionStore to get a proper OAuthSession, then pass that to new Agent().

Fix: Replace lines 163-176 in app/api/oauth/epds/callback/route.ts:
  // WRONG:
  const session = await sessionStore.get(tokenData.sub);
  const agent = new Agent(session as any);
  // CORRECT:
  const oauthSession = await atprotoSDK.restoreSession(tokenData.sub);
  const agent = new Agent(oauthSession);

Import atprotoSDK from @/lib/atproto.

Review of bumicerts-2-4vq.4 and bumicerts-2-4vq.8 found: The DEV_METADATA.client_id served by app/client-metadata.json/route.ts (line 37) is inconsistent with DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId in lib/atproto.ts in two ways:

1. When NEXT_PUBLIC_EPDS_URL is set: DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId includes BOTH redirect_uris (using URLSearchParams.append), but DEV_METADATA.client_id still only embeds ONE redirect_uri (hardcoded string interpolation). Example: DEV_OAUTH_CONFIG.clientId = 'http://localhost?scope=atproto+transition%3Ageneric&redirect_uri=...callback&redirect_uri=...epds/callback' vs DEV_METADATA.client_id = 'http://localhost?scope=atproto%20transition%3Ageneric&redirect_uri=...callback'.

2. Even without ePDS: space encoding differs — URLSearchParams encodes space as '+' but encodeURIComponent encodes it as '%20'. So the strings never match.

This is NOT a runtime bug for loopback clients (ATProto servers parse the client_id URL directly and do not fetch client-metadata.json for loopback clients). However, it violates the principle that client-metadata.json should be the authoritative source of truth for client configuration, and could confuse developers debugging OAuth issues.

Fix: In app/client-metadata.json/route.ts, build DEV_METADATA.client_id using the same URLSearchParams.append approach as lib/atproto.ts:
  const clientIdParams = new URLSearchParams();
  clientIdParams.set('scope', OAUTH_SCOPE);
  for (const uri of redirectUris) {
    clientIdParams.append('redirect_uri', uri);
  }
  const devClientId = 'http://localhost?' + clientIdParams.toString();
  // Use devClientId in DEV_METADATA.client_id

This ensures the client_id string is identical regardless of which file constructs it.

## Files
- app/(marketplace)/_components/Header/HeaderContent.tsx (modify)

## What to do
The `HeaderContent` component has a `useEffect` (line 40-53) with **no dependency array**, causing it to run on every render. It calls `setLeftContent`/`setRightContent`/`setSubHeaderContent` which are `useState` setters in `HeaderProvider`. Since the props (`left`, `right`, `sub`) are JSX elements (new object references on every render), this creates an infinite loop:

1. Render → effect runs → `setRightContent(<JSX>)` → new object → `HeaderProvider` state changes
2. `HeaderProvider` re-renders → children re-render → `HeaderContent` re-renders
3. Go to 1 → **Maximum update depth exceeded**

The error manifests as:
```
Maximum update depth exceeded. This can happen when a component calls setState inside useEffect...
components/actions/oauth.ts (145:23) @ getProfile
```
The stack trace points at `getProfile` because `AtprotoProvider` (which calls `getProfile`) is an ancestor that gets caught in the re-render cascade.

**Fix:** Add `[left, right, sub]` as the dependency array to the first `useEffect`. This way it only runs when the props actually change (by reference).

```tsx
// BEFORE (line 40):
useEffect(() => {
  if (left !== undefined) { setLeftContent(left); ... }
  if (right !== undefined) { setRightContent(right); ... }
  if (sub !== undefined) { setSubHeaderContent(sub); ... }
}); // <-- NO DEPS = runs every render!

// AFTER:
useEffect(() => {
  if (left !== undefined) { setLeftContent(left); ... }
  if (right !== undefined) { setRightContent(right); ... }
  if (sub !== undefined) { setSubHeaderContent(sub); ... }
}, [left, right, sub, setLeftContent, setRightContent, setSubHeaderContent]);
```

**IMPORTANT:** Even with the dep array, JSX props like `right={<Link>...</Link>}` create new references on every parent render. This will still cause unnecessary re-runs. However, it breaks the infinite loop because `setLeftContent(left)` with the same visual output will produce the same state (React batches identical setState calls). If it still loops, the callers need to memoize their JSX props with `useMemo`.

## Don't
- Change the cleanup useEffect (line 56-63) — it correctly has `[]` deps
- Change the HeaderContent API or props interface
- Change any of the callers (ExploreHeader, OrgPageHeader, etc.) unless absolutely necessary to break the loop

## Files
- lib/atproto.ts (modify)

## What to do
Change `resolvePublicUrl()` so it returns a build-time placeholder instead of throwing when no URL can be resolved in a non-development production environment.

### Current behavior (lines 38-57)
The function has a waterfall: NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL → VERCEL_BRANCH_URL → VERCEL_URL → localhost (dev only) → throw. The throw crashes `next build` because the function is called at module level (line 59: `const PUBLIC_URL = resolvePublicUrl()`).

### New behavior
Replace the `throw` at the end (lines 54-56) with:

```typescript
// Build-time fallback — next build evaluates this module at compile time
// when no Vercel env vars are available. Return a placeholder that will
