Review of bumicerts-2-4vq.6 found: The handle resolution in app/api/oauth/epds/callback/route.ts (lines 163-183) constructs an Agent from a raw NodeSavedSession object, which will always fail at runtime.

Evidence: callback/route.ts lines 163-175:
  const session = await sessionStore.get(tokenData.sub);  // returns NodeSavedSession (raw data)
  const agent = new Agent(session as any);  // WRONG: Agent expects OAuthSession with fetchHandler
  await agent.com.atproto.repo.describeRepo(...)  // throws: fetchHandler is not a function

Agent (from @atproto/api) requires an OAuthSession object that has a fetchHandler() method for making authenticated requests. sessionStore.get() returns a NodeSavedSession (plain data object with dpopJwk and tokenSet fields), which has no fetchHandler. The Agent constructor accepts this without error, but any API call immediately throws.

Result: every ePDS login logs a warning and falls back to using the DID as the handle. Users see their DID (e.g., did:plc:abc123) instead of their handle (e.g., user.climateai.org) in the UI.

Fix: Replace sessionStore.get() + new Agent() with atprotoSDK.restoreSession(tokenData.sub), which reads from the session store and returns a proper OAuthSession:
  const oauthSession = await atprotoSDK.restoreSession(tokenData.sub);
  const agent = new Agent(oauthSession);
  const { data: repo } = await agent.com.atproto.repo.describeRepo({ repo: tokenData.sub });

Import atprotoSDK from '@/lib/atproto'. This is the same pattern used in the normal callback (app/api/oauth/callback/route.ts line 33).
